// [Claude, 2026-09-30] Oblique sphere-wall impact extended from the sticking into the sliding regime (tangential/normal
// velocity ratio 0..6 instead of 0..1.5). With mu = 0.3 and e = 0.85, rigid-body impulse theory predicts sliding
// throughout for ratios above (7/2) mu (1 + e) = 1.94. Rerun 2026-09-30: sliding regime (ratio >= 3) within 0.3 %;
// sticking regime deviates by up to 24 % in the spin (tangential spring vs. rigid Coulomb impulse, cf. Chung & Ooi 2011).
// DEM dry-run validation: sphere oblique wall impact vs. the classic rigid-body-with-Coulomb-friction
// analytical solution (AnalyticalSolutions.h::sphereWallImpact), sweeping the tangential/normal
// approach-velocity ratio. Gravity is off -- this is an idealised instantaneous-impact test (a "throw",
// not a "drop"), matching how the reference formula itself treats the impact as instantaneous.
DEMValidation{
    testCase wallImpactSphere;

    sphereRadius 1.0;
    density 1.0;
    restitutionCoefficient 0.85;
    frictionCoefficient 0.3;
    poissonsRatio 0.22;
    collisionTime 0.01;

    impactSpeed 1.0;
    tangentialRatioStart 0.0;
    tangentialRatioEnd 6.0;
    tangentialRatioStep 0.5;

    // 0.25 (not the tighter 0.15 the normal-only case alone would support): mesa_pd's tangential
    // contact law is a Cundall-Strack spring clamped by Coulomb friction, not an idealised
    // instantaneous-impulse Coulomb slide, so induced spin comes in systematically below this
    // analytical prediction at nonzero tangential ratios -- see AnalyticalSolutions.h's calibration
    // note on rigidBodyImpact(). The ratio=0 (pure normal) case alone matches to <1%.
    toleranceRelError 0.25;
    vtkSpacing 0;
    outputBaseName wall_impact_sphere_sliding;
}
